The '''Munda languages''' are a group of closely-related languages spoken by about eleven million people in [[India]], [[Bangladesh]] and [[Nepal]].<ref name="auto">{{Citation |last=Anderson |first=Gregory D. S. |title=Munda Languages |date=29 March 2017 |encyclopedia=Oxford Research Encyclopedia of Linguistics |publisher=Oxford University Press |doi=10.1093/acrefore/9780199384655.013.37 |isbn=978-0-19-938465-5}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |date=23 January 2016 |editor1-last=Hock |editor1-first=Hans Henrich |editor1-link=Hans Henrich Hock |editor2-last=Bashir |editor2-first=Elena |editor2-link=Elena Bashir |title=The Languages and Linguistics of South Asia|doi=10.1515/9783110423303 |isbn=9783110423303 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Santhali |url=https://www.ethnologue.com/language/sat/ |website=Ethnologue |access-date=21 January 2024 |language=en}}</ref> Historically, they have been called the '''Kolarian''' languages.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Anderson |first1=Gregory D. S. |title=The Munda Languages |date=8 April 2015 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-317-82886-0 |page=5 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=6GzxBwAAQBAJ&q=kolarian+languages+munda+languages |language=en}}</ref> They constitute a branch of the [[Austroasiatic languages|Austroasiatic language family]], which means they are distantly related to languages such as the [[Mon language|Mon]] and [[Khmer language|Khmer]] languages, to [[Vietnamese language|Vietnamese]], as well as to minority languages in [[Thailand]] and [[Laos]] and the minority [[Mangic languages]] of [[South China]].<ref>Bradley (2012) notes, MK in the wider sense including the Munda languages of eastern South Asia is also known as Austroasiatic</ref> [[Bhumij language|Bhumij]], [[Ho language|Ho]], [[Mundari language|Mundari]], and [[Santali language|Santali]] are notable Munda languages.<ref name=Sealang>{{cite web|last1=Pinnow|first1=Heinz-Jurgen|title=A comparative study of the verb in Munda language|url=http://sealang.net/sala/archives/pdf4/pinnow1966comparative.pdf|website=Sealang.com|access-date=22 March 2015}}</ref><ref name=elanguage>{{cite web|last1=Daladier|first1=Anne|title=Kinship and Spirit Terms Renewed as Classifiers of "Animate" Nouns and Their Reduced Combining Forms in Austroasiatic|url=http://elanguage.net/journals/bls/article/viewFile/752/645|publisher=Elanguage|access-date=22 March 2015}}</ref><ref name="auto"/>

The family is generally divided into two branches: North Munda, spoken in the [[Chota Nagpur Plateau]] of [[Jharkhand]], [[Chhattisgarh]], [[Bihar]], [[Odisha]] and [[West Bengal]], as well as in parts of [[Bangladesh]] and [[Nepal]], and South Munda, spoken in central Odisha and along the border between [[Andhra Pradesh]] and Odisha.<ref name=Bhattacharya>{{cite journal|last1=Bhattacharya |first1=S. |title=Munda studies: A new classification of Munda |date=1975 |journal=Indo-Iranian Journal |volume=17 |issue=1 |issn=1572-8536 |pages=97–101 |doi=10.1163/000000075794742852|s2cid=162284988 }}</ref><ref name=Languagesgulper>{{cite web|url= http://www.languagesgulper.com/eng/Munda.html |title=Munda languages |website=The Language Gulper |access-date=14 May 2019}}</ref><ref name="auto"/>

North Munda, of which Santali is the most widely spoken and [[Eighth Schedule to the Constitution of India|recognised as an official language in India]], has twice as many speakers as South Munda. After Santali, the Mundari and Ho languages rank next in number of speakers, followed by [[Korku language|Korku]] and [[Sora language|Sora]]. The remaining Munda languages are spoken by small isolated groups and are poorly described.<ref name="auto"/>

General characteristics of the Munda languages include [[Head-directionality parameter|head-final]] clausal structure,{{sfnp|Jenny|Weber|Weymuth|2014|p=61}} the fusion of [[middle voice]] and [[active voice]] with [[tense-aspect-mood]] markers,{{sfnp|Jenny|Weber|Weymuth|2014|p=96}} a distinction between [[clusivity|inclusive and exclusive first-person plural pronouns]] in the pronominal systems,{{sfnp|Jenny|Weber|Weymuth|2014|p=119}} the lack of [[grammatical gender]] as a productive category,{{sfnp|Peterson|2010|p=59}} the widespread use of [[numeral classifiers]],{{sfnp|Peterson|2010|p=61}}{{sfnp|Peterson|2017a|pp=555-556}} and lexical words in referential function ("nouns") all have one invariable form,{{sfnp|Peterson|2010|p=59}} a distinction between alienable and [[inalienable possession]],{{sfnp|Peterson|2010|pp=62-63}}{{sfnp|Peterson|2017a|pp=557}} and rich and pervasive systems of [[auxiliary verbs|auxiliary verb construction]]s (AVCs) in clausal structure.{{sfnp|Anderson|2014|p=394}} The Munda languages are generally [[synthetic language|synthetic]] and [[agglutinative language|agglutinating]].<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Donegan|first1=Patricia Jane|last2=Stampe|first2=David|title=South-East Asian Features in the Munda Languages|url=https://journals.linguisticsociety.org/proceedings/index.php/BLS/article/download/1041/825|journal=Berkley Linguistics Society}}</ref>{{sfnp|Jenny|Weber|Weymuth|2014|p=14}} In Munda sound systems, consonant sequences are infrequent except in the middle of words.

The Munda languages are often interpreted as prime examples of [[father tongue hypothesis|father tongues]] since the majority of native speakers of these languages tend to display the [[Human Y-chromosome DNA haplogroup|Y-chromosome haplogroup]] of the original linguistic [[Founder effect|founding population]] in higher frequencies, and that Y-haplogroup signifies the linguistic origin, rather than based on a [[Human mitochondrial DNA haplogroup|maternal haplogroup]].{{sfnp|van Driem|2021|p=186}}

==Origin==
Many linguists suggest that the [[Proto-Munda language]] probably split from [[Proto-Austroasiatic]] somewhere in [[Indochina]].{{Citation needed|date=July 2024}} Studies by Chaubey et al. (2011), Arunkumaret al. (2015), Metspalu
et al. (2018), and Tätte et al. (2019) all show that the Munda branch of the Austroasiatic family was created as the result of a male-biased linguistic intrusion into the [[Indian subcontinent]] from [[Southeast Asia]] during the Late [[Neolithic]] period (Sidwell & Rau 2019 cited Tätte et al. (2019), estimate a date of formation between 3,800 and 2,000 years ago), which carried the [[Y chromosome|paternal lineage]] [[Haplogroup O-M175|O1b1a1a]] into India from either [[Meghalaya]] or the sea.{{sfnp|van Driem|2021|p=186}} These studies and analyses confirm [[George van Driem]]'s Munda Father tongue hypothesis.{{sfnp|van Driem|2021|p=187}} [[Paul Sidwell]] (2018) suggests they arrived on the coast of modern-day [[Odisha]] about 4000–3500 years ago ({{circa|2000|1500}} BCE) and spread after the [[Indo-Aryan migration]] to the region.<ref>Sidwell, Paul. 2018. [https://www.academia.edu/36689736/Austroasiatic_Studies_state_of_the_art_in_2018 Austroasiatic Studies: state of the art in 2018]. Presentation at the Graduate Institute of Linguistics, National Tsing Hua University, Taiwan, 22 May 2018.</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Sidwell AA studies state of the art 2018.pdf |url=https://drive.google.com/file/d/1O3SkrYNq20ZmUgcK-MFPN7QAvByEMfVA/view?usp=embed_facebook |access-date=12 May 2023 |website=Google Docs}}</ref>

Rau and Sidwell (2019),<ref>{{cite journal|author1=Rau, Felix|author2=Sidwell, Paul|author-link2=Paul Sidwell|title=The Munda Maritime Hypothesis|date=2019|issn=1836-6821|journal=Journal of the Southeast Asian Linguistics Society |volume=12|issue=2|hdl=10524/52454}}</ref><ref>Rau, Felix and Paul Sidwell 2019. "The Maritime Munda Hypothesis." ICAAL 8, Chiang Mai, Thailand, 29–31 August 2019. {{doi|10.5281/zenodo.3365316}}</ref> along with Blench (2019),<ref>Blench, Roger. 2019. [http://rogerblench.info/Language/Austroasiatic/Munda%20hypothesis%202019.pdf The Munda maritime dispersal: when, where and what is the evidence?]</ref> suggest that Pre-Proto-Munda had arrived in the [[Mahanadi River Delta]] around 1500 BCE from Southeast Asia via a maritime route, rather than overland. The Munda languages then subsequently spread up the [[Mahanadi]] watershed. 2021 studies suggest that Munda languages impacted [[Eastern Indo-Aryan languages]].<ref>Ivani, Jessica K; Paudyal, Netra; Peterson, John (2021). Indo-Aryan – a house divided? Evidence for the east–west Indo-Aryan divide and its significance for the study of northern South Asia. Journal of South Asian Languages and Linguistics, 7(2):287–326. {{doi|10.1515/jsall-2021-2029}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/355078227|title=The spread of Munda in prehistoric South Asia -the view from areal typology To appear in: Volume in Celebration of the Bicentenary of Deccan College Post-Graduate and Research Institute (Deemed University)|author=John Peterson|date=October 2021|access-date=1 September 2022}}</ref>
[[File:Austroasiatic languages map.svg|thumb|Present-day distribution of Austroasiatic languages]]
[[File:Austro-asiatic dispersal map.jpg|thumb|Austro-asiatic dispersal map]]

==Classification==
===Internal classification===

====Diffloth (1974)====
The bipartite [[Gérard Diffloth|Diffloth]] (1974) classification is widely cited:

{{tree list}}
*'''Munda'''
**North Munda
*** [[Korku language|Korku]]
***[[Kherwarian languages|Kherwarian]]
****Kherwari branch: [[Birjia language|Birjia]], [[Koraku language|Koraku]]
****Mundari branch: [[Mundari language|Mundari]], [[Bhumij language|Bhumij]], [[Asur language|Asuri]], [[Koda language|Koda]], [[Ho language|Ho]], [[Birhor language|Birhor]], [[Kol language (Bangladesh)|Kol]], [[Turi language|Turi]]
****Santal branch: [[Santali language|Santali]], [[Mahali language|Mahali]]
**South Munda
***Kharia–Juang: [[Kharia language|Kharia]], [[Juang language|Juang]]
***Koraput Munda
**** Remo branch: [[Gata language|Gata]] (Gta), [[Bondo language|Bondo]] (Remo), [[Bodo Gadaba language|Bodo Gadaba]] (Gutob)
****Savara branch [Sora–Juray–Gorum] : [[Parengi language|Parengi]] (Gorum), [[Sora language|Sora]] (Savara), [[Juray language|Juray]], [[Lodhi language|Lodhi]]
{{tree list/end}}

====Diffloth (2005)====
Diffloth (2005) retains Koraput (rejected by Anderson, below) but abandons South Munda and places Kharia–Juang with the northern languages:

{{clade
|label1=Munda
|1={{clade
   |label1=Koraput
   |1={{clade
      |1=[[Bonda language|Remo]]
      |2=[[Sora language|Savara]]
      }}
   |label2=Core&nbsp; &nbsp;Munda
   |2={{clade
   |1=[[Kharia language|Kharia]]–[[Juang language|Juang]]
   |label2=North&nbsp; &nbsp;Munda
   |2={{clade
   |1=[[Korku language|Korku]]
   |2=[[Kherwarian languages|Kherwarian]]
      }}
      }}
    }}
}}

====Anderson (1999)====
[[Gregory Anderson (linguist)|Anderson]]'s 1999 proposal is as follows.<ref name="Anderson1999">Anderson, Gregory D.S. (1999). "A new classification of the Munda languages: Evidence from comparative verb morphology." Paper presented at 209th meeting of the American Oriental Society, Baltimore, MD.</ref>

{{tree list}}
*'''Munda'''
**North Munda
***[[Korku language|Korku]]
***[[Kherwarian languages|Kherwarian]]: [[Santali language|Santali]], [[Mundari language|Mundari]]
**South Munda (3 branches)
***Kharia–Juang: [[Juang language|Juang]], [[Kharia language|Kharia]]
***Sora–Gorum: [[Sora language|Sora]], [[Gorum language|Gorum]]
***Gutob–Remo–Gtaʔ
****Gutob–Remo: [[Gutob language|Gutob]], [[Bonda language|Remo]]
****[[Gtaʼ language|Gtaʼ]]:{{sfnp|Anderson|2008a|p=682}} Plains Gtaʔ, Hill Gtaʔ
{{tree list/end}}

However, in 2001, Anderson split Juang and Kharia apart from the Juang-Kharia branch and also excluded Gtaʔ from his former Gutob–Remo–Gtaʔ branch. Thus, his 2001 proposal included five branches for South Munda.

====Anderson (2001)====
Anderson (2001) follows Diffloth (1974) apart from rejecting the validity of Koraput. He proposes instead, on the basis of morphological comparisons, that Proto-South Munda split directly into Diffloth's three daughter groups, Kharia–Juang, Sora–Gorum (Savara), and Gutob–Remo–Gtaʼ (Remo).<ref>{{cite book
|last=Anderson
|first=Gregory D S
|title=A New Classification of South Munda: Evidence from Comparative Verb Morphology
|series=Indian Linguistics
|volume=62
|year=2001
|publisher=Linguistic Society of India
|location=Poona
|pages=21–36
}}</ref>

His South Munda branch contains the following five branches, but the North Munda branch is the same as those of Diffloth (1974) and Anderson (1999).

{{blockquote|[[Sora language|Sora]]–[[Gorum language|Gorum]] [[Juang language|Juang]] ↔ [[Kharia language|Kharia]] ↔ [[Gutob language|Gutob]]–[[Bonda language|Remo]] ↔ [[Gta language|Gtaʔ]]}}

*''Note'': "↔" = shares certain innovative isoglosses (structural, lexical). In Austronesian and Papuan linguistics, this has been called a "[[Linkage (linguistics)|linkage]]" by [[Malcolm Ross (linguist)|Malcolm Ross]].

====Sidwell (2015)====
[[Paul Sidwell]] (2015){{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=197}} considers Munda to consist of 6 coordinate branches, and does not accept South Munda as a unified subgroup.

{{tree list}}
*'''Munda'''
**North Munda
***[[Korku language|Korku]]
***[[Kherwarian languages|Kherwarian]] ([[Santali language|Santali]], [[Mundari language|Munda]])
**[[Sora language|Sora]]–[[Gorum language|Gorum]]
**[[Juang language|Juang]]
**[[Kharia language|Kharia]]
**[[Gutob language|Gutob]]–[[Bonda language|Remo]]
**[[Gtaʼ language|Gtaʔ]]
{{tree list/end}}

===External classification===
Traditionally, the Austroasiatic phylum was divided into two primary groups, Mon-Khmer and Munda, following [[Wilhelm Schmidt (linguist)|Schmidt]] (1906)'s classification. [[Thomas Sebeok|Sebeok]] (1942) and [[Heinz-Jürgen Pinnow|Pinnow]] (1959, 1960, 1963) later reinforced this model, attributing the distinction to the typological contrast between the geographically eastern Mon-Khmer (Pinnow's Khmer-Nicobar) and the South Asian Munda branch.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=167}} This binary classification, however, overlooks lexical evidence, the effects of [[language contact]] and [[Language convergence|areal convergence]] that may have profoundly shaped the structure of individual branches. Pinnow (1963:150) himself acknowledged that his classification "[...]have only structural and geographical justification."{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=167}} As comparative-historical research progressed, alternative hypotheses concerning the external position of Munda emerged. During the 1970s and 1980s, [[Gérard Diffloth|Diffloth]] and [[Harry L. Shorto|Shorto]] independently came up with a hypothesis that suggests recognizing the phylum as made up of two Northern and Southern phylogenetic groups on the basis of shared lexical innovations and the '''*s-:*t-''' correspondence in minor syllables.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=168}} Under this hypothesis, Munda formed part of the Northern group together with [[Khasian languages|Khasian]], [[Palaungic languages|Palaungic]], [[Khmuic languages|Khmuic]], [[Mang language|Mang]], and [[Vietic languages|Vietic]]. [[Paul Sidwell|Sidwell]] (2014), however, argues that Munda lacks convincing lexical and structural innovations linking it to this putative Northern subgroup.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=169}} He further notes that the lexical isoglosses required to unite Mang-Pakanic with the [[Khasi–Palaungic languages|Khasi-Palaungic]] group are also absent.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|pp=171–172}}

A computational phylogenetic analysis study conducted by Sidwell & Greenhill (2014) found no evidence supporting a higher-level Mon-Khmer v. Munda division.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=165}} Instead, the Austroasiatic family was optimally resolved into eleven primary nodes, with Munda recovered as a coherent coordinate branch equivalent in rank to the other major lineages.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=179}} The analysis therefore provides no support for treating Munda as the sister group of a monophyletic Mon-Khmer subgroup.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=166}}

==Sociolinguistics and history of Munda studies==
[[File:LSI map of Munda languages.jpg|thumb|[[George Abraham Grierson|Grierson's]] Linguistic Map of India, 1906]]
The Munda languages are the least documented languages in [[South Asia]].{{sfnp|Anderson|2008|p=Preface}} Most Munda languages, except [[Santali language|Santali]] with ~8 million native speakers, can be tentatively described as under threat, with [[Mundari language|Mundari]] (~1.5 million), [[Ho language|Ho]] (~1 million), [[korku language|Korku]] (~400,000), [[Kharia language|Kharia]] (~200,000), and [[Sora language|Sora]] (~250,000–300,000) still maintaining substantial everyday usage; smaller South Munda languages such as [[Juang language|Juang]], [[Gtaʼ language|Gtaʔ]], [[Bonda language|Remo]], [[Birhor language|Birhor]], and [[Gorum language|Gorum]] survive in small, fragmented populations amidst more than half a billion [[Indo-Aryan languages|Indo-Aryan]] and [[Dravidian languages|Dravidian]] speakers in one of the most [[Indo-Gangetic Plain|densely populated regions on Earth]].{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=5}} Speakers are concentrated in central and [[eastern India]], especially [[Jharkhand]], [[Odisha]], [[West Bengal]], [[Madhya Pradesh]], [[Maharashtra]], and adjacent regions, typically in hill and forest zones,{{sfnp|Anderson|2008|p=1}} which Peterson (2017) terms ''accretion zones''.{{sfnp|Peterson|2021|pp=5–6}} [[Literacy rates]] are usually low among speakers, and opportunities for daily communication to be written down are largely restricted to the larger languages. [[Multilingualism]] is widespread, with frequent use of Hindi, [[Bengali language|Bengali]], [[Nagpuri language|Sardi]], [[Odia language|Odia]], [[Marathi language|Marathi]], [[Telugu language|Telugu]], and other regional languages in administration, trade, and education.{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=7}}

Munda languages have long been affected by social stigmas because their speakers, mainly belonging to Adivasi, Dalit, and other socially marginalized communities, have historically occupied subordinate positions within the regional caste and socioeconomic hierarchy. Although [[oral literature|folk literatures]] are abundant, it is not so surprising that the Munda languages lack [[writing|written]] [[literature]] before the 19th century;{{sfnp|Anderson|2008|p=6}} the same can be said of other languages spoken by [[dalit]] and [[Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes|tribal communities]] (''adivasi'') of South Asia ([[Brahui language|Brahui]], [[Kurukh language|Kurukh]], [[Bhili language|Bhili]], [[Burushaski language|Burushaski]], [[Nihali language|Nihali]], etc). Colonial and postcolonial attitudes often viewed tribal populations as "primitive" and backward, and these perceptions became attached to their languages, which dominant groups frequently regarded as rural and lacking prestige.{{sfnp|Anderson|2008|p=5}} The dominance of regional Indo-Aryan languages such as Hindi, [[Bengali language|Bengali]], [[Odia language|Odia]], and [[Nagpuri language|Sardi]] in education, administration, religion, and public life further reinforced this hierarchy, constantly pressuring many Munda speakers to shift toward socially prestigious majority languages.{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=5}}

Long-term intense contact has led to extensive Aryanization. Some communities formerly associated with Munda languages have largely abandoned them and now speak Indo-Aryan varieties instead. [[Turi language|Turi]] is a clear example: although it is genetically a North Munda language, most [[Turi (caste)|ethnic Turi]] no longer speak it fluently with just over 0.56% of the Turi population use the language in daily life.{{sfn|Peterson|Minz|Linda|Khan|2024|p=264}} Birhor communities, meanwhile, increasingly adopt [[Nagpuri language|Sardi]] and Hindi.{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=7}} Similar pressures affect Kharia-speaking regions, where prolonged interaction with neighboring Indo-Aryan populations and Kherwarian groups has produced strong lexical and structural convergence. In several South Munda communities, monolingual speakers have disappeared entirely.{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=7}} Certain Indo-Aryan speaking groups in the lowland plain may have originally Munda speakers who went through Aryanization, such as the [[Chero]]s of [[Bihar]], the [[Lodhi language|Lohar]] weavers of [[Ranchi]],{{sfnp|Anderson|2008|p=6}} the [[Musahar]]s and [[Tharu people|Tharus]] of lowland Nepal, and the [[Sahariya]] of Eastern [[Uttar Pradesh]].{{sfnp|Peterson|2021|p=14}}

The contact situation certainly is not one-directional, however. Some large Munda languages, like [[Kherwarian languages|Kherwarian]] Mundari and Santali, have also been adopted by neighboring non-Munda populations through close social and economic interaction. The Dravidian [[Kurukh people|Kurukh]]s of [[Ranchi]] adopted a [[Mundari language|Mundari]] dialect called Keraʔ Mundari, and certain low-caste Indo-Aryan communities in the Ranchi region also speak Mundari as a community language.{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=8}} As a result, ethnic and linguistic identities do not always align neatly in eastern and central India. The modern sociolinguistic landscape of the Munda family is therefore shaped simultaneously by language loss, [[Cultural assimilation|assimilation]], [[bilingualism]], and localized language expansion through contact. Following [[Johanna Nichols|Nichols]]' (1992, 1997) conceptual distinction between spread zones and accretion zones, Peterson (2021) argues that the present distribution of the Munda languages largely reflects their survival in residual hilly regions rather than their original extent. In the prehistoric era, egalitarian Munda-speaking and pre-Munda-speaking agricultural communities might have once dominated much of the Eastern [[Gangetic Plains]] and adjacent regions before the expansion of Indo-Aryan languages by at least 600 BCE.{{sfnp|Peterson|2021|p=15}} Subsequent period of intensive interaction with non-Munda speakers, Munda speakers, and Indo-Aryan speakers in the spread zone of the plains may have resulted in large-scale [[language shift]], forming the Jharkhand-Bihar-West Bengal linguistic area, clearly aligns with the Indo-Aryan [[Eastern Indo-Aryan languages|East]]-West morphosyntactical split today, as Munda speakers were assimilated or reduced to accretion zones of the mountains and hills where their languages and tribal identities survived.{{sfnp|Peterson|2021|pp=11–12}}{{sfnp|Peterson|2021|p=16}}

As for the reconstructed history of the Munda languages, researchers primarily rely on verbal morphology to define Munda classification. Lexical and phonological features are also significant, but they tend to reflect their respective regional linguistic areas and are thus less reliable indicators of phylogeny. Verbal morphology is far less likely to be diffused and requires long-term intimate contact between different ethnolinguistic groups.{{sfnp|Anderson|2008|p=3}} From the morphological perspective, it is pretty straightforward that the Munda languages are divided into six coordinate branches: North Munda, [[Sora language|Sora]]-[[Juray language|Juray]]–[[Gorum language|Gorum]], [[Gutob language|Gutob]]-[[Bonda language|Remo]], [[Kharia language|Kharia]], [[Juang language|Juang]], and [[Gtaʼ language|Gtaʔ]].{{sfnp|Anderson|2020|p=160}} Previous scholars emphasized a North-South Munda division;{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=195}} however, with new clues suggested by scholars such as David Stampe and Felix Rau, it has become more evident that the North-South divide may have never existed.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=197}} The contiguous North Munda continuum remained stable with relatively minimal diversification for a long period until a northward expansion of Dravidian-speaking groups such as the [[Gonds]], [[Kurukh people|Kurux]], and [[Malto people|Malto]] likely cut off the land bridge between the Korku and the Kherwarians.{{sfnp|Rau|Sidwell|2019b|pp=37–38}} Some morphological similarities shared between Kharia and Remo-Gutob may be attributed to a period of common development of their proto-forms. At some point in distant history, Proto-Gtaʔ perhaps came under areal influence of Proto-Gutob-Remo.{{sfnp|Anderson|2020|p=159}} Otherwise the non-North Munda branches are very old, reflecting closer to Proto-Munda structure than North Munda languages.{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=1}}

[[File:Pagan races of the Malay Peninsula (1906) (14781207342).jpg|thumb|right|An early map of the Austroasiatic languages (known as Mon-Annam then) by [[Charles Otto Blagden|Blagden]], 1906]]

Researchers had already recognized the Austroasiatic nature of the Munda languages even before the Austroasiatic hypothesis itself was first proposed by Logan (1856). In 1848, [[Brian Houghton Hodgson|Hodgson]] noticed that the so-called "Kol languages" (Ho, Santali) of Bengal constitute a third major linguistic family of India alongside Indo-Aryan and Dravidian, marking the first explicit genetic classification of the group. Few years later, British man [[James Richardson Logan|James Logan]] (1850) and American [[Francis Mason (missionary)|Francis Mason]] (1854) independently advanced the genetic connection between [[Ho language|Ho]] and [[Mon language|Mon]] and [[Vietnamese language|Vietnamese]].{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=150}} Two years later, in 1856, Logan proposed placing the "Kolarian languages" within the "Mon-Annam" language family, which was progressively developed by prominent researchers such as [[Robert Needham Cust|Cust]] (1878), [[Ernst Kuhn|Kuhn]] (1889), [[Charles Otto Blagden|Bladgen]] (1894), [[George Abraham Grierson|Grierson]] (1906), and [[Wilhelm Schmidt (linguist)|Schmidt]] (1906) into the [[Austroasiatic language family]].{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=151}} Despite this, there have been doubts and skepticism regarding their genetic status. 

Typologically, the Munda languages are structurally abnormal, not only within their own [[Austroasiatic]] phylum but also in comparison with their Indo-Aryan and Dravidian neighbors,{{sfnp|Anderson|2021|p=25}} although in certain respects it is clear that Munda has areally converged with Indo-Aryan and Dravidian within the macro [[Indosphere|South Asian linguistic area]],{{sfnp|Jenny|Weber|Weymuth|2014|p=138}}{{sfnp|Anderson|2014|p=373}}{{sfnp|Anderson|2014|p=398}}{{sfnp|Anderson|2021|pp=64–65}} which implies an East Asian origin of Munda in a region not too far removed from the [[Sino-Tibetan languages|Sino-Tibetan]] and [[Austronesian languages|Austronesian]] homelands.{{sfnp|Sagart|2022|p=323}} Munda shows strong traces of structural influence and lexical borrowings from a non-Indo-Aryan, non-Dravidian [[Stratum (linguistics)|substrate]], probably [[Substratum in Munda languages|extinct contact languages]] that might have been dominant in the Eastern [[Gangetic Plains]] in prehistoric times before Austroasiatic pre-Proto-Munda arrival.{{sfnp|Rau|Sidwell|2019b|p=41}}{{sfnp|Anderson|2021|pp=56–59}}{{sfnp|Anderson|2021|p=65}} Those substrate-influenced anomalies in Munda are so profound that many linguists find it difficult to ignore them. Some scholars in the past actually disagreed with Schmidt's Austroasiatic classification of Munda and even proposed alternative hypotheses. In a series of papers published between 1928 and 1934, Hungarian linguist [[Wilhelm von Hevesy]] attacked Schmidt's Austroasiatic classification and suggested a [[Finno-Ugric]] affiliation of Munda with an apparent Mon-Khmer substratum instead.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|p=153}} In an approach based on structural typology in 1942, [[Thomas Sebeok]], despite refuting Hevesy's Munda-Finno-Ugric hypothesis, he rejected any genetic connection between Munda and Vietnamese.<ref>{{cite journal |url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/409554|jstor=409554 |title=An Examination of the Austroasiatic Language Family |last1=Sebeok |first1=Thomas A. |journal=Language |year=1942 |volume=18 |issue=3 |pages=206–217 |doi=10.2307/409554 |url-access=subscription }}</ref>{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|pp=154–156}} Doubts about the Austroasiatic classification were not considerably eliminated until the works of German linguist [[Heinz-Jürgen Pinnow]] (1954, 1959), who began establishing systematic Austroasiatic lexical comparative studies and [[Proto-Munda language|Proto-Munda]] reconstructions,{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=9}} providing the first substantial addition to Schmidt's work and the first morphological reconstruction of Proto-Munda, thus codifying Austroasiatic linguistics as a major field of linguistic study since the first hypothesis emerged in the 1850s.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2014|pp=158–159}}

==Phonology==
===Consonants, vowels, and syllable structure===
The Munda languages share similar sets of [[phonemes]] with regional languages in their respective areas.{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|pp=2-3}} Inherited Austroasiatic "checked" [[glottalization|glottalised stop]] (pre-glottalised articulatory) and [[nasalised]] final consonants found in some Munda languages such as [[Mundari language|Mundari]] (eg. ''ub'' ("hair") is realised as [uˀb̥ᵐ]) and [[Kharia language|Kharia]] (eg. ''oreˀdʒ'' ("ox") is realised [ɔrɛˀɟ˺ⁿ]) may stand out in South Asia.{{sfnp|Anderson|2014|p=378}} One key feature in the Munda consonants is the distinction between dental and [[retroflex]] stops (/t̪/ vs /ʈ/, /d̪/ vs /ɖ/) in lexical level exists in most Munda languages except [[Sora language|Sora]] and [[Gorum language|Gorum]], reflecting a general characteristic of South Asian ([[Indosphere]]) phonology.{{sfnp|Jenny|Weber|Weymuth|2014|p=24}} Because of South Asian areal convergence, Munda languages generally have fewer vowels (between 5 and 10) than their Eastern Austroasiatic relatives.{{sfnp|Jenny|Weber|Weymuth|2014|p=30}}{{sfnp|Sidwell|Rau|2014|p=314}} Additionally, [[Sora language|Sora]] has glottalised vowels. Like any other Austroasiatic languages, the Munda languages make extensive uses of [[diphthongs]] and [[triphthongs]]. Larger vowel sequences can be found, with an extreme example of [[Santali language|Santali]] ''kɔeaeae'' meaning ‘he will ask for him’.{{sfnp|Anderson|2014|p=375}} Most Munda languages lack [[Tone (linguistics)|tones]] with an exception of [[Korku language|Korku]], which has acquired two contrastive tones within the South Asian linguistic area: an unmarked high and a marked low,{{sfnp|Zide|2008|p=258}}{{sfnp|Sidwell|Rau|2014|p=318}} in which the low tone might have emerged from the putative laryngeal feature *<sup>χ</sup> in a transitive past suffix of Proto-Munda.{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=13}} The general syllable shape is (C)V(C).{{sfnp|Sidwell|Rau|2014|p=312}} Initial clusters of the prototypical Austroasiatic [[sesquisyllabic|sesquisyllables]] C(C)CVC mostly appear as resyllabified in Munda languages, except [[Gtaʼ language|Gtaʔ]] which is the only Munda language that allows initial clusters, eg. ''gsæŋ'' "chicken."{{sfnp|Sidwell|Rau|2014|p=320}}{{sfnp|Sidwell|2015|p=75}} However, scholars skeptically consider that Gtaʔ sesquisyllabic structure might have been the result of a local secondary development.{{sfnp|Sidwell|2015|p=77}}

As stated above, [[tonogenesis]] in Korku and continuous CCVC/[[sesquisyllabic]] development in Gtaʔ, both of which were unfolded inside the [[Indosphere|South Asian linguistic area]], which is very hostile to tone and initial clusters, seem to be local innovations and have nothing related to contact-driven restructuring in the subcontinent.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Zide|first1=Norman H.|editor-last1=Zide|editor-first1=Norman H.|title=Studies in comparative Austroasiatic linguistics, 214-1000|chapter=Korku Low Tone and the Proto-Korku-Kherwarian Vowel System|publisher=Mouton|location=London—The Hague—Paris|date=1966|pages=214–229}}</ref> It is also unclear whether they had anything connected to areal convergences in the Eastern Austroasiatic languages of [[Mainland Southeast Asia]].{{sfnp|Sidwell|2015|p=77}} Munda word shape is dictated by a general phonotactical phenomenon called [[bimoraic constraint]], which requires free-standing nominal stems to stay disyllabic or to obtain weight at the stressed syllable; that is, monosyllabic free form of lexemes must be expanded (i.e. resyllabified and/or reduplicated) in order to remain heavy (Anderson & Zide 2001).{{sfnp|Jenny|Weber|Weymuth|2014|p=16}}<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Sagart|first=Laurent |author-link=Laurent Sagart|date=September 2011 |title=How many independent rice vocabularies in Asia ?|url=https://thericejournal.springeropen.com/articles/10.1007/s12284-011-9077-8|journal=Rice|volume=4 |issue=1 |pages=121–133|doi=10.1007/s12284-011-9077-8}}</ref>{{sfnp|Sidwell|Rau|2014|p=316}} See [[#Vocabulary]] for comparison.

===Munda phonemes===
The following table compiles lists of consonantal and vowel systems of several Munda languages, mainly from {{harvtxt|Anderson|2014}}, {{harvtxt|Nagaraja|1999}}, and many others on [[International Phonetic Alphabet]].{{sfnp|Anderson|2014|pp=376-377}}<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Khalid|first=Zoya|date=1 August 2020|title=A Phonological Sketch of Asur|journal=Language in India|volume=20|issue=8|pages=252–268|issn=1930-2940}}</ref><ref>{{Cite thesis |last=Shamim |first=Ahmed |title=A Description of the Phonology and Morphology of Kɔɖa, An Endangered Language of Bangladesh |date=June 2021 |degree=PhD |publisher=City University of New York |url=https://academicworks.cuny.edu/gc_etds/4413}}</ref>{{sfn|Peterson|Minz|Linda|Khan|2024|p=267}}<ref>{{Cite book|last=Nagaraja|first=K. S.|year=1999|title=Korku language: grammar, texts, and vocabulary|publisher=Institute for the Study of Languages and Cultures of Asia and Africa, Tokyo University of Foreign Studies}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last1=Pradhan|first1=Sabita|last2=Tripathi|first2=Arimardan Kumar|last3=Bhagat|first3=Akash|year=2022|title=Birhor: Language and life|publisher=Centre for Endangered Languages, Visva-Bharati, Santiniketan-731235|isbn=978-81-957226-6-2}}</ref>

