===Word prominence===
{{harvtxt|Donegan|Stampe|2004}} posited overarching assumptions that all Munda languages have completely redesigned their word [[Prosody (linguistics)|prosodic]] structure from proto-Austroasiatic rising [[intonation (linguistics)|intonation]], [[Iamb (poetry)|iambic]] and reduced vowel, sesquisyllabic structure to Indic norms of [[trochee|trochaic]], falling rhythm, stable or assimilationist consonants and harmonised vowels, making them typological opposed to Eastern Austroasiatic languages at almost every level. {{harvtxt|Sidwell|Rau|2014}} criticised Donegan & Stampe by pointing out that the overall picture appears much more complicated and diverse and that generalisations of Donegan & Stampe are not supported by the instrumental data of the various Munda languages.{{sfnp|Sidwell|Rau|2014|p=321}} {{harvtxt|Peterson|2011b}} describes word-rising contour in monosyllables and second syllable prominence in [[Kharia language|Kharia]] content words. Even the presence of [[clitics]] and [[affixes]] does not drive Kharia word prosodic structure to that of a trochaic and falling system. {{harvtxt|Osada|2008}} reports final-syllable stress in all but CVC.CV stems in [[Mundari language|Mundari]].{{sfnp|Osada|2008|p=104}} {{harvtxt|Horo|Sarmah|2015}}, Horo (2017) and {{harvtxt|Horo|Sarmah|Anderson|2020}} found that the [[Sora language|Sora]] disyllables are always iambic, reduced first syllable vowel space, and second syllable prominence.<ref>{{cite journal|surname1=Horo|given1=Luke|surname2=Sarmah|given2=Priyankoo|title=Acoustic analysis of vowels in Assam Sora|journal=North East Indian Linguistics|year=2015|volume=7|issue=|pages=69–88}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|surname1=Horo|given1=Luke|surname2=Sarmah|given2=Priyankoo|surname3=Anderson|given3=Gregory D. S.|title=Acoustic phonetic study of the Sora vowel system|journal=The Journal of the Acoustical Society of America|year=2020|volume=147|issue=4|pages=3000–3011|doi=10.1121/10.0001011|pmid=32359268 |bibcode=2020ASAJ..147.3000H }}</ref> Even CV.CCə words show final-syllable prominence. {{harvtxt|Horo|Sarmah|2015}} note that the Sora vowels of the first syllables are centralised and that vowels in the second syllables are more representative of the canonical vowel space.

{{harvtxt|Ghosh|2008}} describes about [[Santali language|Santali]] prosody that "stress is always released in the second syllable of the word regardless of whether it is an open or a closed syllable".{{sfnp|Jenny|Weber|Weymuth|2014|p=38}}{{sfnp|Anderson|2014|p=379}} His analysis was confirmed by {{harvtxt|Horo|Anderson|2021}}, whose acoustic data clearly shows that the second syllable in Santali is always the prominent syllable, with a greater intensity of [[stress (linguistics)|stress]] and a rising [[contour (linguistics)|contour]].<ref>{{cite book|last1=Horo|first1=Luke|last2=Anderson|first2=Gregory D. S.|chapter=Towards a prosodic typology of Kherwarian Munda languages: Santali of Assam.|title=Papers from the 30th Annual Meeting of the Southeast Asian Linguistics Society. JSEALS Special Publications 8|editor-first1=Mark J.|editor-last1=Alves|editor-first2=Paul|editor-last2=Sidwell|publisher=University of Hawai'i Press|date=2021|pages=298–317}}</ref>

{{harvtxt|Zide|2008}} reports that in [[Korku language|Korku]], the final syllable is heavier than the initial syllable, and within a disyllable, stress is preferentially released at the final syllable.{{sfnp|Zide|2008|p=260}} The analyses inferred from fieldworks demonstrate that despite exhibiting some variants, most Munda prominence alignments are in line with other Austroasiatic languages, with a predictable final-syllable prominence in a prosodic word. Again, {{harvtxt|Donegan|Stampe|2004}}'s claim on rhythmic holism does not conform with the data presented by individual Munda languages.{{sfnp|Hildebrandt|Anderson|2023|p=559}}

==Morphology==
Morphologically, both North and South Munda subgroups mainly focus on the head or the verb, thus they are primarily [[head-marking]], in contrast to the [[Indo-European languages|Indo-European]] and [[Dravidian languages|Dravidian]] languages, which are mainly [[dependent-marking]].{{sfnp|Donegan|Stampe|2004|p=13}} As a result, nominal morphology is less complex than is verbal morphology.{{sfnp|Sidwell|Rau|2014|p=311}}{{sfnp|Anderson|2014|p=381}} [[Grammatical case|Case markers]] on nominals to show [[morphosyntactic alignment|syntactic alignments]] ([[nominative-accusative]] or [[ergative-absolutive]]) are largely absent or not systematically developed in the Munda languages, especially the Kherwarian lects. The relation between subject and object in clause is conveyed mainly through verbal referent indexation and [[word order]]. At the clause/sentence level, Munda languages are [[head-final]] but internally [[head-first]] in referent indexation, compounds, and [[noun incorporation]] verb complexes.{{sfnp|Donegan|Stampe|2002|p=115–116}}{{sfnp|Jenny|2020|p=24}}

Munda head-first, bimoraic constraint-free noun incorporation is also found in [[Khasic languages|Khasian]], [[Nicobarese languages|Nicobaric]], and other Mon-Khmer languages.{{sfnp|Anderson|2007|p=205}}{{sfnp|Anderson|2016|p=121}} In [[Morphological derivation|word derivation]], besides their own innovative methods, the Munda languages maintain Austroasiatic methods in forms of reduplication, compounding, and derivational [[infixes|infixation]] and [[prefixes|prefixation]].{{sfnp|Anderson|2014|p=382}}

One unusual characteristic that appears to be pervasive among the Munda languages is lexical flexibility, that is, a large number to almost entire their lexicons are ''precategorial'', i.e. ''lexically underspecified'' for categories such as [[noun]], [[verb]], [[adjectives]] etc. Thus, these languages may rely more on syntax and affixes/clitics to distinguish [[parts of speech]]. Pinnow summarised the issue back in the 1960s,<ref>{{cite book|last1=Pinnow|first1=Heinz-Jürgen|editor-last1=Zide|editor-first1=Norman H.|title=Studies in comparative Austroasiatic linguistics|chapter=A comparative study of the verb in the Munda languages|publisher=Mouton|location=London—The Hague—Paris|date=1966|pages=96–193}}</ref> 

{{quotation|[...]Theoretically any word for any concept, i.e., all words, can function as a verb base. Thus, we may not speak of a verb in the Indo-European sense. This fact was recognized at an early date and is now generally known. This is true for all Munda languages, but cannot be considered distinctive of them since there are innumerable other language families in which the situation is the same.[...] This phenomenon undoubtedly goes back to very ancient times and can probably be accepted as Proto-Munda.|Heinz-Jürgen Pinnow, 1966:101}} 

The following elicited examples from [[Simdega district|Simdega]] Kharia illustrate the problem:

{{Interlinear|indent=3|'''lebu''' ɖel{{=}}ki|man come{{=}}MID.PST|'The/A '''man''' came.'}}

{{Interlinear|indent=3|Bhagwan '''lebu'''{{=}}ki ro ɖel{{=}}ki|God '''man'''{{=}}MID.PST and come{{=}}MID.PST|'God '''became man''' (Jesus) and came (to earth).'}}

The degree of lexical flexibility are extremely prominent in North Munda and [[Kharia language|Kharia]], where the lexicons may only contain an open class of contentives; whereas in other South Munda languages this phenomenon however tends to be much weaker.{{sfnp|Rau|2013|p=170}}

===North Munda===
The North Munda subgroup is split between Korku and the 14 Kherwarian languages.

====Kherwarian languages====
[[Kherwarian languages|Kherwarian]] is a large language continuum with speakers extending west to east from the Indian states of [[Uttar Pradesh]] to [[Assam]], north to south from [[Nepal]] to [[Odisha]]. They include fourteen languages: [[Asur language|Asuri]], [[Birhor language|Birhor]], [[Bhumij language|Bhumij]], [[Koda language|Koda]], [[Ho language|Ho]], [[Korwa language|Korwa]] (Korowa), [[Mundari language|Mundari]], [[Mahali language|Mahali]], [[Santali language|Santali]], [[Turi language|Turi]], [[Agariya language|Agariya]], [[Birjia language|Bijori]], [[Koraku language|Koraku]], and [[Santali language|Karmali]], with the total number of speakers surpassing ten million (2011 census). The Kherwarian languages are often highlighted because their elaborate and complex templatic and pronominalised predicate structures are so pervasive that it is obligatory for the verb to encode [[tense–aspect–mood]], [[Voice (grammar)|voices]], [[Transitivity (grammar)|transitivity]], and indexation of two arguments, including outside arguments like possessors.

